DEFINITION  Homo sapiens NUT midline carcinoma family member 1 (NUTM1),
            transcript variant 1, mRNA.
ACCESSION   NM_001284292 XM_005254276
VERSION     NM_001284292.1
KEYWORDS    RefSeq; RefSeq Select.
SOURCE      Homo sapiens (human)
  ORGANISM  Homo sapiens
            Eukaryota; Metazoa; Chordata; Craniata; Vertebrata; Euteleostomi;
            Mammalia; Eutheria; Euarchontoglires; Primates; Haplorrhini;
            Catarrhini; Hominidae; Homo.
REFERENCE   1  (bases 1 to 4111)
  AUTHORS   Schaefer IM, Dal Cin P, Landry LM, Fletcher CDM, Hanna GJ and
            French CA.
  TITLE     CIC-NUTM1 fusion: A case which expands the spectrum of
            NUT-rearranged epithelioid malignancies
  JOURNAL   Genes Chromosomes Cancer 57 (9), 446-451 (2018)
   PUBMED   29700887
  REMARK    GeneRIF: Fluorescence in situ hybridization and targeted
            next-generation sequencing identified a CIC-NUTM1 fusion resulting
            from t(15;19)(q14;q13.2). In light of morphologic features that
            overlap with those of NC from typical anatomical sites we have seen
            previously, the tumor was best classified as falling within the NC
            spectrum rather than CIC-associated sarcoma.
REFERENCE   2  (bases 1 to 4111)
  AUTHORS   Dickson BC, Sung YS, Rosenblum MK, Reuter VE, Harb M, Wunder JS,
            Swanson D and Antonescu CR.
  TITLE     NUTM1 Gene Fusions Characterize a Subset of Undifferentiated Soft
            Tissue and Visceral Tumors
  JOURNAL   Am. J. Surg. Pathol. 42 (5), 636-645 (2018)
   PUBMED   29356724
  REMARK    GeneRIF: Report subset of undifferentiated soft tissue and visceral
            tumors with NUTM1 gene fusions.
REFERENCE   3  (bases 1 to 4111)
  AUTHORS   Giridhar P, Mallick S, Kashyap L and Rath GK.
  TITLE     Patterns of care and impact of prognostic factors in the outcome of
            NUT midline carcinoma: a systematic review and individual patient
            data analysis of 119 cases
  JOURNAL   Eur Arch Otorhinolaryngol 275 (3), 815-821 (2018)
   PUBMED   29356890
  REMARK    GeneRIF: Review and case series found that NUT midline carcinoma
            has dismal prognosis. Radiotherapy and chemotherapy improves
            survival, but do not provide long term control except in anecdotal
            cases. No impact on overall survival was found based on type of
            molecular translocation, i.e., NUT-BRD4, NUT-BRD3 or other
            variants.
            Review article
REFERENCE   4  (bases 1 to 4111)
  AUTHORS   Lee JK, Louzada S, An Y, Kim SY, Kim S, Youk J, Park S, Koo SH,
            Keam B, Jeon YK, Ku JL, Yang F, Kim TM and Ju YS.
  TITLE     Complex chromosomal rearrangements by single catastrophic
            pathogenesis in NUT midline carcinoma
  JOURNAL   Ann. Oncol. 28 (4), 890-897 (2017)
   PUBMED   28203693
  REMARK    GeneRIF: we also identified a complex genomic rearrangement
            involving the BRD4-NUT rearrangement underlying the simple t(15;19)
            karyotype in Nuclear protein in testis (NUT) midline carcinoma
REFERENCE   5  (bases 1 to 4111)
  AUTHORS   Zee BM, Dibona AB, Alekseyenko AA, French CA and Kuroda MI.
  TITLE     The Oncoprotein BRD4-NUT Generates Aberrant Histone Modification
            Patterns
  JOURNAL   PLoS ONE 11 (10), e0163820 (2016)
   PUBMED   27698495
  REMARK    GeneRIF: We postulate that BRD4-NUT (B4N) complexes override the
            preexisting histone code with new posttranslational modifications
            patterns that reflect aberrant transcription and that
            epigenetically modulate the nucleosome environment toward the
            NUT-midline carcinoma (NMC) state.
REFERENCE   6  (bases 1 to 4111)
  AUTHORS   Stelow EB and French CA.
  TITLE     Carcinomas of the upper aerodigestive tract with rearrangement of
            the nuclear protein of the testis (NUT) gene (NUT midline
            carcinomas)
  JOURNAL   Adv Anat Pathol 16 (2), 92-96 (2009)
   PUBMED   19550370
  REMARK    GeneRIF: Some carcinomas of the upper aerodigestive tract have a
            rearrangement of the nuclear protein of the testis (NUT) gene (NUT
            midline carcinomas).
            Review article
REFERENCE   7  (bases 1 to 4111)
  AUTHORS   Stelow EB, Bellizzi AM, Taneja K, Mills SE, Legallo RD, Kutok JL,
            Aster JC and French CA.
  TITLE     NUT rearrangement in undifferentiated carcinomas of the upper
            aerodigestive tract
  JOURNAL   Am. J. Surg. Pathol. 32 (6), 828-834 (2008)
   PUBMED   18391746
  REMARK    GeneRIF: Approximately, 20% of undifferentiated carcinomas of the
            upper aerodigestive tract not associated with EBV infection were
            found to have rearrangements of NUT by FISH.
REFERENCE   8  (bases 1 to 4111)
  AUTHORS   French CA, Ramirez CL, Kolmakova J, Hickman TT, Cameron MJ, Thyne
            ME, Kutok JL, Toretsky JA, Tadavarthy AK, Kees UR, Fletcher JA and
            Aster JC.
  TITLE     BRD-NUT oncoproteins: a family of closely related nuclear proteins
            that block epithelial differentiation and maintain the growth of
            carcinoma cells
  JOURNAL   Oncogene 27 (15), 2237-2242 (2008)
   PUBMED   17934517
  REMARK    GeneRIF: BRD-NUT fusion proteins contribute to carcinogenesis by
            associating with chromatin and interfering with epithelial
            differentiation.
REFERENCE   9  (bases 1 to 4111)
  AUTHORS   Haruki N, Kawaguchi KS, Eichenberger S, Massion PP, Gonzalez A,
            Gazdar AF, Minna JD, Carbone DP and Dang TP.
  TITLE     Cloned fusion product from a rare t(15;19)(q13.2;p13.1) inhibit S
            phase in vitro
  JOURNAL   J. Med. Genet. 42 (7), 558-564 (2005)
   PUBMED   15994877
  REMARK    GeneRIF: Wild type Brd4 inhibits G(1) to S progression and we also
            found that the Brd4-NUT fusion augments the inhibition of
            progression to S phase compared with wild type Brd4.
REFERENCE   10 (bases 1 to 4111)
  AUTHORS   French CA, Miyoshi I, Kubonishi I, Grier HE, Perez-Atayde AR and
            Fletcher JA.
  TITLE     BRD4-NUT fusion oncogene: a novel mechanism in aggressive carcinoma
  JOURNAL   Cancer Res. 63 (2), 304-307 (2003)
   PUBMED   12543779
